EMK wrote:

 > After my first excursion into the game and finding
 > that I was dead of starvation within 10 minutes
 > (real time),

You only get 300 or so game turns on the nourishment
state in which you start, so you'll need to eat food
you have, or find and eat food, or eat the corpse of
a safe to eat monster, somewhere along in there and
regularly thereafter.

If you died in ten minutes of real clock time, that
means you were moving about one turn every two
seconds. Try moving one turn every ninty seconds and
see what it does for your playing success. NetHack
is a puzzle, which takes solving, not a twitch
reflex game, which takes out-reacting.

 > I figure that I need to get a better handle on how
 > time works in this game.

Yes, that's crucial. It comes both with study and
with experience.

 > For instance, I get that moving one space is
 > considered a "turn," but how long is that exactly?

NetHack is more complex than that, but essentially,
your question misses the essence of things: nothing
happens, no time passes, until you press a key, so a
turn is a turn is a turn, end of story -- no, but
wait, there's more...

NetHack is _purely_ a turn based game, like
checkers, chess, or go.

As to your "exactly", that's very complicated.

You can be in varioius speed statuses: very fast,
fast, normal, slowed, burdened, strained, and
stressed, and overburdened, if I got that right,
plus mounted or afoot or floating.

If mounted, you move at the speed of your mount.

At each of those complexly determined "speeds", you
are given some number of "movement points" _per
turn_, and consume them inversely per speed, so that
at "very fast" you consume the least movement points
per turn, and at stressed you consume the most.
(because you can't move at all at "overburdened")
and *things work the same way for all the monsters*,
and your and the monsters' movement point
consumption rates per abstract turn need not match.

Moreover, the movement points roll-over between
turns, so that unused movement points in one turn
can be used in the next. This turns out to be
crucial to understand for fighting some kinds of
slow and dangerous monsters, like mimics.

After this, a ritual of "who has movement points
ready to use" affects who gets to move first in each
turn, monster or PC. and there may be many monsters
within reach of the PC, and their speed and the
"going first" outcomes may be different for each, so
that you're PC is being hit when you thought it was
your turn to do the hitting.

That's why more experienced NetHackers try to avoid
hitting (being in "melee") unless they they can do
it with zero risk.

The previous response's URLed wikia entry on "Turn"
is useful, but this one is even more so:

http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Speed

 > And what other actions/commands take a "turn" to
 > complete?  Which don't take any time at all?  Are
 > there any that take some fraction of a turn?

Yes to the last, and the above two URLed wikia
entries explain one by one which actions consume
turns, and some of the rules are quite weird.

 > This seems like it would be important to know.

Yes, you are unlikely ever to progress far at
NetHack unless you learn its mechanics.

 > - If I'm standing near some monster and decide to
 > (s)earch for hidden doors/traps am I giving that
 > monster enough time to pounce on me?

Yep, searching takes time, so he may well get a turn.
Searching lots of times takes lots of time, so he'll
surely get a turn. The good news is that when he
"whops you upside the noggin" you stop searching.

 > How about if I just take a good look at him with
 > the "/" key?

Free lunch, yippee. Notice that both ';' and '/'
work as "take a look at" commands, the first one
does one look and quits, the second one keeps on
letting you look until you tell it to stop, usually
by typing a period when the search indicator empty
rectangle is on an empty bit of floor.

 > Or what if I put on that helmet I found in the
 > other room?

Putting on or taking off armor costs time, sometimes
a lot of time, and stuff you expect to take more
time usually does, like suits of armor a lot, gloves
a little, but you'll spend something like at least a
turn for any bit of armor.

 > If I've been though the level and can't find the
 > stairs to the next level (why I died the on the
 > first level), I know that what I need to do is
 > (s)earch for a hidden door or passage I missed.

Well, you also need to look under any movable
objects, too, especially statues, they all can hide
a staircase. Step on the object and do ':' to look
under you for a staircase. Try not to grab a
cockatrice corpse barehanded that way, though.

Usually doing that "check under stuff" _first_ is
the proper approach, before going on a hidden door
search, but if a big chunk of the level has
obviously gone missing, and you haven't heard sounds
of a vault guard to explain that empty spot, it's
nearly certain there are more rooms on the level
than you've seen

 > I see two ways to do this:

 > 1. Step-n10s to search each space 10 times before
 > moving on to the next space or

 > 2. Step-s to search each space once before giving
 > the whole area a second sweep.  Which would be
 > more efficient game-time-wise?

Neither. A search covers the wall bit in front of
you plus the wall bits on each side of you, so you
only have to search every third wall bit. Your
second method would be worse, since you take more
steps to do the same amount of searching on the same
amount of wall. If I recall correctly, "7" is the
anticipated number of searches to find a hidden door
or hidden extension of a passage, but "30" isn't all
that uncommon, so I usually do my wall dancing and
type '9s' at each stop (because it is one less
character to type than '10s').

I'll do the dance around each room on the level in
turn, then go back and do the rooms all again, so
that I don't waste all my time searching in a room
where there's nothing to find.

It also helps to know that you can pray to your
deity for help, but very infrequently, if you get in
many kinds of trouble including starving. Eating a
few times between prayers seems to do wonders.

It also helps to know that a passage going to an
empty part of the level map can often start on a side
of a room opposite to the empty space you want to
reach.

